<p><code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">MemoryLayout&lt;Type&gt;</code> is a generic type evaluated at compile time. It’s used to determine the size, alignment, and stride of the specified <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">Type</code>, returning values in bytes.</p>

<p>For example, <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">Int16</code> has a size of 2 bytes and an alignment of 2. This means it must start at an even address — an address divisible by 2.</p>

<p>For example, an <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">Int16</code> can be placed at address 100, but not at address 101 — the odd address violates the required alignment.</p>

<p>When you pack a bunch of <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">Int16</code> values together, they’re laid out at intervals of the stride. For these basic types, the stride is the same as the size.</p>

