<p>This example is similar to the previous one, except that it creates a raw pointer first. You create a typed pointer by binding the memory to the desired type, <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">Int</code>.</p>

<p>By binding the memory, you can access it in a type-safe way. When you create a typed pointer, the memory binding happens behind the scenes.</p>

<p>The rest of this example is also the same as the previous one. Once you’re in typed-pointer territory, you can use features like <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">pointee</code>.</p>

<h3 id="getting-the-bytes-of-an-instance">Getting the Bytes of an Instance</h3>
<p>Often, you already have an instance of a type and want to inspect the bytes that make it up. You can use the <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">withUnsafeBytes(of:)</code> method to do this.</p>

<p>To do so, add the following code to your Playground:</p>

<div class="language-swift highlighter-rouge"><div class="highlight"><pre class="highlight"><code><table class="rouge-table"><tbody><tr><!-- <td class="rouge-gutter gl"><pre class="lineno">1
