<p>You declare the file variable as <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">static</code>, so only one instance exists in the system. You’ll rely on the system to close it when the process exits.</p>

<p>Since multiple threads may need random numbers, you need to protect access to it with a serial GCD queue.</p>

<p>The <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">get</code> function is where the real work happens.</p>

<p>First, create an uninitialized storage that’s one larger than you need, because <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">fgets</code> always terminates with <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">\0</code> (null character).</p>

<p>Next, work within the GCD queue to get data from the file.</p>

<p>Finally, copy the data into a standard array by wrapping it in an <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">UnsafeMutableBufferPointer</code> (which can act as a sequence).</p>

<p>So far, this only safely provides you with an array of <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">Int8</code>. Now, you’ll extend it.</p>

